## INTRODUCTION
Metal−organic frameworks are a well-known class of porous materials comprised of inorganic units bridged by coordinating organic linkers. In addition to possessing high internal surface areas, their physical and chemical properties can be tuned for specific applications by judicious choice of the metal center and organic ligand. These properties lead to applications in storing and separating gases, in which it is particularly important to have precise control over the strength and specificity of interactions between the pore surface and various potential adsorbates.1 Indeed, strong adsorption sites are often installed on the pore surface in order to attract specific gas molecules selectively for separation applications or to increase the density of gas molecules present for storage applications.1h,j,2
Exposed metal cations represent an important example of strong adsorption sites that have been realized in many metal− organic frameworks.3 These Lewis acidic sites, which are typically formed by removing metal-coordinated solvent molecules upon heating under reduced pressure, are highly
polarizing and have strong interactions with many small gas molecules.3a,4 For example, the well-established \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) (M = Mg, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, Zn; \(\mathrm{dobdc}^{4-}=2,5\)-dioxido-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate) structure type, also known as M-MOF-74, CPO-27-M, or \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dhtp})\),5 contains a high density of exposed metal cations and is especially promising for the storage of a wide variety of gases. As a result of its compact, highly charged tetraanionic linker, this framework is endowed with an exceptionally high density of open metal coordination sites, which are the primary binding sites for small gas molecules such as \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\), and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Consequently, \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) frameworks have been investigated for numerous potential applications involving gas adsorption, including \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage,3a,4a,c,6 methane storage,4b,7 \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture,8 and hydrocarbon separations.1i,9 All of these applications take advantage
of strong interactions between gas molecules and exposed metal cations.
Discovering new frameworks with a high density of open metal coordination sites is not trivial, as it is difficult to predict what topologies will form with partial solvation of the metal nodes and will then further be amenable to desolvation. Rather, selectively tuning the most promising of the numerous existing frameworks by altering the linker, the metal, or both provides a viable strategy for developing promising new adsorbents. Since the \( \mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \) series of frameworks has high chemical and thermal stability\(^{8b,10}\) and has been shown to be outstanding for binding a high capacity of small gas molecules, we hoped to modify this framework by tuning the electronics of the exposed metal cations, which should subsequently tune the affinity for different gas molecules. For example, the binding of \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) or \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \) might be enhanced by increasing the positive charge density at the metal cation site, thus increasing its ability to polarize and bind adsorbing gas molecules more strongly. Furthermore, \( \mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \) offers the advantage of being an isostructural series of frameworks that can be formed with a variety of metals, thereby offering an additional level of control for tuning the framework for specific interactions. Based on the high density of exposed metal cations, isostructural nature of the these frameworks, and thermal stability, a combination of properties that is not paralleled by any other structure type, \( \mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \) provides an ideal platform for exploring the tunability of metal−organic frameworks to strengthen framework−gas interactions.
This work outlines the synthesis of a structural isomer of the \( \mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \) framework, which will be referred to as \( \mathrm{M}_{2}(m-\mathrm{dobdc}) \) ( \( m \)-dobdc\(^{4-} \) = 4,6-dioxido-1,3-benzenedicarboxylate). Rather than having para carboxylic acid functionalities and para hydroxyl substituents, as in the regular \( \mathrm{H}_{4} \)dobdc linker, \( \mathrm{H}_{4}(m-\mathrm{dobdc}) \) has meta carboxylic acid groups and meta hydroxyl substituents. The \( \mathrm{H}_{4}(m-\mathrm{dobdc}) \) linker was targeted due to its ease of synthesis and potentially low cost. Indeed, a solvent-free Kolbe−Schmitt reaction to produce \( \mathrm{H}_{4}(m-\mathrm{dobdc}) \) using only resorcinol, \( \mathrm{KHCO}_{3} \), and \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) is efficient and inexpensive. In spite of this linker isomerism, it is still possible to form a framework with a similar overall structure, replete with one-dimensional hexagonal channels and a high density of open metal coordination sites. This structural isomerism takes advantage of the fact that \( \mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \) frameworks have multiple types of coordinating functional groups in the linker, which is a rarity among most well-known metal−organic frameworks.
The resulting new framework has subtle differences in the electronic structure of the ligand and overall connectivity as compared to the \( \mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \) framework, which might be expected to influence interactions with small gas molecules by changing the local environment around the open metal coordination sites. Isomers of metal−organic frameworks are known and primarily derive from what are termed "framework isomers." The structure of \( \mathrm{M}_{2}(m-\mathrm{dobdc}) \), however, is an example of a ligand-originated isomer.\(^{11}\) Relatedly, a recent \( \mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \) analogue was synthesized with thiols rather than phenols,\(^{12}\) but a purely structural isomer of \( \mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \) has never before been synthesized.
Here, \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) adsorption is used as a probe to determine whether this new framework exhibits enhanced gas adsorption properties. The choice of \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) as a probe molecule originates from its simplicity, low polarizability, and potential use as a clean, renewable fuel.\(^{2a,13}\) Through the use of \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) adsorption isotherms, powder neutron diffraction, inelastic neutron
scattering, infrared spectroscopy, and first-principles electronic structure calculations, we provide a careful comparison of the differences between \( \mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}) \) and \( \mathrm{M}_{2}(m-\mathrm{dobdc}) \) that contribute to the differences in their \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) adsorption properties.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Methanol was purchased from commercial vendors, further dried over molecular sieves, and deoxygenated by purging with \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \). All other reagents were purchased from commercial vendors and used without further purification, unless otherwise noted. The \( \mathrm{H}_{4}(m-\mathrm{dobdc}) \) ligand was synthesized according to a literature procedure.\(^{14}\)
Synthesis of \( \mathbf{M}_{2}(m\mathbf{-}\mathbf{dobdc}) \) \( \mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n} \), Fe, Co, Ni). Anhydrous \( \mathrm{MCl}_{2} \) (5.6 mmol) and \( \mathrm{H}_{4}(m\mathrm{-dobdc}) \) (2.4 mg, 1.2 mmol) were added to \( 80~\mathrm{~mL} \) of a mixed solvent \( \left(x\% \right.\) MeOH by volume in dimethylformamide (DMF), where for \( \mathrm{Mn},\,x=15;\,\mathrm{Fe},\,x=15;\,\mathrm{Co}, \) \( x=50; \) Ni, \( x=35 \) ) in a nitrogen-filled glovebox for \( \mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Mn} \), Fe and in air for \( \mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Co} \), Ni. The solution was dispensed into eight \( 20\,\mathrm{\mL} \) scintillation vials, which were each sealed with a PTFE-lined cap and heated at \( 120\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 18\,\mathrm{~h~} \). The resulting solid from each vial was combined, submerged in \( 20\;\mathrm{mL} \) of DMF, and heated at \( 70\,^{\circ}\!C \) for \( 24\,\mathrm{{h}} \). The DMF was decanted and replaced with \( 20~\mathrm{mL} \) of methanol. The resulting suspension was heated at \( 70\,^{\circ}\!C \) for 4 days, during which time the methanol was replaced every \( 24~\mathrm{h} \). The material was activated by heating it at \( 150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) under dynamic vacuum on a Schlenk line for \( 12\,\mathrm{{h}} \), followed by further activation of a small amount of the sample by heating the solid under dynamic vacuum \( (<\!10\;\mu\mathrm{bar}) \) at \( 180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) (160 \( ^{\circ}C \) for \( \mathrm{Fe}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc}) \) ) for \( 24~\mathrm{h} \) at a ramp rate of \( 0.5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C/min} \). It should be noted that larger scale syntheses of \( \mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc}) \) and \( \mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc}) \) were also accomplished with no loss of crystallinity or surface area by stirring at room temperature in a round-bottom flask equipped with a reflux condenser at a concentration of \( 20.2\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{H}_{4}(m\mathrm{-dobdc}) \) and \( 50.5\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{MCl}_{2} \) \( \mathbf{\nabla}M=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o} \), Ni) in \( 1250~\mathrm{mL} \) solvent. Times and temperatures were identical to the small-scale synthesis, but \( 200~\mathrm{mL} \) of solvent was used for each exchange.
Synthesis of \( \mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}(m\mathbf{-}\mathbf{dobdc}) \). This compound was synthesized in air in a round-bottom flask by adding \( 300\;\mathrm{mg} \) of \( \mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) and \( 93~\mathrm{mg} \) of \( \mathrm{H}_{4}(m\mathrm{-dobdc}) \) to \( 14~\mathrm{mL} \) MeOH in \( 31~\mathrm{mL} \) DMF and stirring at \( 120\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( ^{\mathrm{~8~h~}} \), then filtering off the resulting solid. Detailed characterization of all frameworks is contained in the Supporting Information.
Physical Measurements. Thermogravimetric analyses were carried out at a ramp rate of \( 2~{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min} \) under a \( 25~\mathrm{mL/min}~\mathrm{N}_{2} \) flow with a TA Instruments TGA Q5000. Infrared spectra were collected on a PerkinElmer Avance Spectrum 400 FTIR spectrometer equipped with a Pike attenuated total reflectance (ATR) accessory. Diffraction data were collected with \( 0.02^{\circ} \) steps using a Bruker AXS D8 Advance diffractometer equipped with \( \mathrm{Cu-K}\alpha \) radiation \( (\lambda\ =\ 1.5418\ \mathrm{\AA}) \), a Göbel mirror, a Lynxeye linear position-sensitive director, and mounting the following optics: fixed divergence slit \( (0.6\ \mathrm{\mm}) \), receiving slit \( (3\,\mathrm{\mm}) \), and secondary beam Soller slits \( (2.5^{\circ}) \). The generator was set at \( 40\;\mathrm{kV} \) and \( 40\;\mathrm{mA} \). Samples were either loaded on zero background sample holders or packed into air-free capillaries in a nitrogen-filled glovebox and mounted using a capillary stage. Elemental analyses were obtained from the Microanalytical Laboratory of the University of California, Berkeley.
Oil-free vacuum pumps and oil-free pressure regulators were used for all measurements. Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) and Langmuir surface areas were determined from \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption data at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) using Micromeritics software.
Inelastic Neutron Scattering. Inelastic neutron scattering (INS) spectra were collected using the Filter Analyzer Neutron Spectrometer (FANS)15 at the NCNR on the same samples used for the NPD experiments. Spectra were obtained at \(7\,\mathrm{K}\) using the pyrolytic graphite (002) monochromator and \(20^{\prime}{-}20^{\prime}\) collimation options. Data were first collected for the bare framework, followed by data collection for the sample loaded with normal- \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) \(\left(n\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)\), which contains a 3:1 mixture of ortho \(\left(o\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)\) to para \(\left(p\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)\), respectively. For \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\), data were collected at loadings of 0.33, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, and \(4.0\,\mathrm{~n-H}_{2}\) molecules per Co atom, while for \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) data were collected at loadings of 0.67, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, and \(4.0\,\mathrm{~n-H}_{2}\) molecules per Ni atom. Gas was loaded into the materials using the same methodology as described in the NPD experiments. The spectra of the bare frameworks were subtracted from the spectra obtained from the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) loaded samples, and Gaussian peaks were fit to the rotational transitions using the DAVE suite of programs.16 Further measurements of the framework vibrational densities of states for the activated \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) material were made to higher energies (35 to 160 meV) using the \(\mathrm{Cu}(220)\) monochromator with \(20^{\prime}{-}20^{\prime}\) collimation.
Infrared Spectroscopy. Infrared spectra were acquired using a Bomem DA3Michelson interferometer equipped with a glowbar source, \(\mathrm{CaF}_{2}\) beamsplitter, and a liquid nitrogen cooled mercury−cadmium-telluride detector. A cutoff filter above \(9000\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was used to prevent unwanted sample heating from the IR source. A custom-built diffuse reflectance system17 with a sample chamber that allows both the temperature and atmosphere of the material to be controlled was used for all experiments. Powder samples of the frameworks \((\sim\!10\) mg) were transferred under inert atmosphere to a cup affixed to a copper slab providing thermal contact to a coldfinger cryostat (Janis ST-300T). The sample temperature was monitored by a Si-diode thermometer. Known quantities of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) gas were dispensed from a calibrated gas manifold by monitoring the change in pressure.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Structural Characterization of \(\mathsf{M}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\). A less expensive regioisomer of \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{dobdc}\) was selected in order to form a framework with the same overall topology and a high density of open sites as in \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\), but with potentially different local geometry and electronic properties. After the synthesis of \(\mathrm{H}_{4}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) from resorcinol, it was possible to synthesize an isostructural series of \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) \((\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g},\) Mn, Fe, Co, or Ni) frameworks (Figure 1) exhibiting powder
X-ray diffraction patterns (Figures S1−S5) analogous to the respective \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) frameworks, indicating that the two series adopt similar structures. Thermogravimetric analyses of the \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) frameworks show initial mass losses of \(33\%\) and \(14\%\), respectively, indicative of volatilization of trapped solvent molecules and thus porosity (Figure S6a). After repeatedly washing each compound with methanol to replace the metal-bound DMF, the frameworks were activated by heating under dynamic vacuum at \(180\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Thermogravimetric analysis of the activated frameworks indicated that \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) are stable to \(250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and the \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) is stable to \(350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
from \(C_{2h}\) in \(\mathrm{H}_{4}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) to \(C_{2\nu}\) in \(\mathrm{H}_{4}(m\) -dobdc), a change in the space group from R3 in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) to \(R3m\) in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) is observed.4a Additional structural differences between the two compounds are apparent.9c For instance, the orientation of the carboxylate groups of the linker is changed, with the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}{}^{-}\) unit twisting out of the plane of the aromatic ring by approximately \(12.5^{\circ}\) in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc), as compared to just \(3.5^{\circ}\) in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) . Furthermore, metal centers in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) that face into the same pore align directly along the crystallographic \(b\) axis, resulting in a \({\mathrm{Co}}_{2}{\cdots}{\mathrm{Co}}\) separation of 14.9(1) Å across the channels. In \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) , the metals are offset from each other by one-third of a twist in the chain, leading to a \({\mathrm{Co}}{\cdots}{\mathrm{Co}}\) separation of 15.24(8) Å across the channels. A structureless Le Bail refinement of the other \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\cdot\) - dobdc) \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g},\) Mn, Fe, Ni) compounds afforded related unit cells, confirming that the entire series is isostructural.
With such similar crystal structures, the surface areas would be expected to be similar for the two types of frameworks. Indeed, a geometric approximation of the surface areas for \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) predicts them to both be 1297 \(\mathbf{m}^{2}/\mathbf{g}\) .23 Low-pressure \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms collected at 77 K reveal type I isotherms characteristic of microporous solids for the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) \(\left(\mathbf{M}\ =\ \mathbf{M}\mathbf{n},\right.\) Fe, Co, Ni) compounds (Figure S11). Langmuir and BET surface areas were calculated (Table 1), and in each case the results are comparable to those reported for \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) .4b,c,9e,24 Based on the similar framework structure, this indicates full evacuation of the pores and complete desolvation of the framework. Furthermore, the \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) BET surface area of \(1264~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) is very close to the predicted surface area of \(1297\,\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) from the geometric calculations. Achieving complete activation of the \(\mathrm{Mg}\) analogue
has proven challenging, and all efforts have thus far resulted in materials with lower than expected surface areas. As a result, the remaining data will focus only on the other four \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) compounds.
\(\mathbf{H}_{2}\) Adsorption Isotherms. To probe the potentially modified electronic structures at the open metal coordination sites, low-pressure \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms were measured for the four \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n},\) Fe, Co, Ni) frameworks at 77 and \(87\,\mathrm{~K~}\) (Figures S20−S23). As for the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) series,4a \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) has the greatest \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake at \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\) and 1 bar, followed by Fe, Mn, and Co compounds, respectively. Although the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) frameworks have a higher \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake at 1 bar, in the very low-pressure regime, \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) has a significantly higher uptake than \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) at both 77 and \(87\,\mathrm{~K~}\) (Figure S27). This indicates that the interaction of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) with the exposed \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) cations ion the surface of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) is stronger than in \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) ; since these frameworks have about the same surface area, another effect must be influencing the binding strength of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) .
Isosteric heats of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption were calculated in order to gain insight into this increased low-pressure \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption and confirm whether there is indeed a stronger interaction between \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and the metal centers in the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) frameworks. In order to determine the isosteric heat of adsorption \(\left(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\right)\) using the Clausius−Clapeyron relation, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) isotherm data at 77 and \(87\,\mathrm{~K~}\) were fit with either a dual-site Langmuir−Freundlich equation or a trisite Langmuir equation. Similar to \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}),^{4a}\) the isosteric heat of adsorption plots for \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\) - dobdc) imply a nearly constant \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding enthalpy until a loading of \(0.7{-}0.8\ \mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{M}^{2+}\) , followed by a sharp decrease as all exposed metal cations become occupied and only weaker adsorption sites are available (Figure 13). The inflection points
range from \({\sim}0.75\ \mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{M}\) in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) to \({\sim}0.85\ \mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{M}\) in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(m\) -dobdc), which corresponds with the fraction of metal sites available for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding. These values are comparable to previously observed values for the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) series.24
Increasing the binding enthalpy of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in metal−organic frameworks is important for practical applications, as a binding enthalpy of \(-15\) to \(-20\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) is predicted to be optimal for the on-board storage of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at ambient temperatures.25 The low-coverage isosteric heats of adsorption of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(m\) -dobdc), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(m\) -dobdc), \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\) -dobdc), and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\) -dobdc) are \(-10.3,\) \(-11.1,\) \(-11.6,\) and \(-12.3\,\mathrm{~kJ/mol},\) respectively. This trend in binding enthalpies for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is consistent with the Irving−
Williams series, which predicts that high-spin complexes increase in stability moving from Groups 7 to 10.26 The trend further mirrors that observed for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding within \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) frameworks (Table 2). Calculated isosteric heat of
adsorption values are highly sensitive to the fitting equation used, the temperatures at which data are collected and the quality of the fits. Given this, the values for the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) series were calculated from isotherms collected in this work (except \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\), which was previously reported by our group and fit using the same method)4c in order to maintain consistency in the collected isotherms and the manner in which they are fit. These values all agree with the literature values for the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) series except for \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\), which we found to be \(1\;\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) less than the literature value of \(-12.9\;\mathrm{kJ/mol}\).4a This discrepancy most likely arises from a difference in data collection, isotherm temperatures, and equation used to fit the data. Consequently, our data that were collected and fit in the same manner as the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) data presented here are used for comparison. Importantly, the isosteric heats of adsorption for the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) compounds are, on average, \(\sim1.0\;\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) stronger than in the corresponding \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) frameworks
(Table 2) and are as much as \(1.5\;\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) stronger in the case of \(\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{n}}\), which also has the largest percent increase \((17\%)\) in binding enthalpy.
Increasing the \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\) loading in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) revealed several secondary binding sites. At a loading of \(1.25\;\mathrm{D}_{2}\) per metal, a second binding site (site II) adjacent to the primary site becomes populated (Figure 4b). This can be attributed to a \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\cdots\mathrm{D}_{2}\) interaction based on a short \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\cdots\mathrm{D}_{2}\) separation of \(2.88(4)\;\mathrm{\AA}\), combined with a \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\cdots\mathrm{O}\) interaction occurring at a
separation of 3.28(6) Å from the nearest framework oxygen atom. Site II is in a similar location to the secondary binding sites observed in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) and \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\),<sup>6c</sup> whereby the second bound \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\) appears to rely on \(\mathrm{D}_{2} \cdots \mathrm{D}_{2}\) interactions in all cases. The site I to site II \(\mathrm{D}_{2} \cdots \mathrm{D}_{2}\) separation, however, of 3.05(2) Å in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) at loading of \(2.25 \mathrm{D}_{2}\) per metal is significantly shorter than the 3.16(2) Å observed at the same loading in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) or the 3.16(8) Å arising for a similar loading of \(1.2 \mathrm{D}_{2}\) per metal in \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\). This closer site I to site II separation in \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) is most likely a polarization effect, as the more strongly bound \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\) residing at site I has a larger induced dipole, leading to a stronger interaction with a \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\) molecule adsorbed at site II.
Neutron diffraction experiments intended to elucidate binding sites are generally performed using \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\) instead of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) because the large incoherent scattering cross-section of \(\mathrm{H}\) atoms contributes strongly to the background and reduces data quality. In addition, the coherent scattering cross-section of a D atom is approximately four times larger than that of an H atom. While it has been shown through gas adsorption measurements that metal−organic frameworks tend to adsorb \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\) slightly more strongly than \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\),<sup>28</sup> it has been assumed that both molecules behave similarly when binding, only displaying extremely minor differences in interaction distances. In order to test this assumption, we measured and successfully refined the structure of \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) dosed with a low loading of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\). We believe this is the first structural data from powder neutron diffraction reported for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in a metal−organic framework. As expected from the \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\) data, a dose below \(1.0 \mathrm{H}_{2}\) per metal site resulted in binding exclusively at site I. Interestingly, the center of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) density was found to be 2.26(4) Å from the Co atom, perhaps slightly longer than the \(\mathrm{Co} \cdots \mathrm{D}_{2}\) distance of 2.23(5) Å, although still within the associated error. Whether this is indicative of a slightly stronger interaction with \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\) is somewhat unclear due to the resolution of the data; however, it does confirm that \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\) can be expected to behave similarly for the purposes of structural determination.
Inelastic Neutron Scattering. Inelastic neutron scattering (INS) experiments were carried out to probe the site-specific binding properties of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\). Data for various loadings of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) after subtraction of the spectra for the evacuated materials are shown
in Figure 5. At loadings up to and including \(1.0 \mathrm{~n-H}_{2}\) (corresponding to a 3:1 ortho-\(\mathrm{H}_{2}\):para-\(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) ratio) molecule per
metal atom, two low-energy rotational lines are apparent at 7.8(1) and 9.5(1) meV for \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) and 7.5(1) and 9.3(1) meV for \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\). These features are similar to those observed in INS spectra for several compounds in the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) series at low loadings and have been assigned to transitions occurring from the \(J=0\) state to sublevels of the split \(J=1\) rotational state for initial \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules adsorbed at the metal centers.<sup>4c,6b,c,24</sup> This assignment has been confirmed through correlation with neutron diffraction and DFT calculations.<sup>29</sup> Presumably, there is also a higher-energy transition, not collected within this current data range, as seen in the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) series. The splitting between the low-energy peaks of approximately \(1.6 \mathrm{meV}\) for both \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) is smaller than that observed for any of the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) materials. The position of the first peak is at higher energies than that of the first peak of the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) frameworks (except for the Zn analogue), and the position of the second peak is at lower energies than is observed for all the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) compounds. The energy splitting of these peaks has been previously shown to have no correlation with the binding strength of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at the open metal site.<sup>30</sup>
At loadings above \(0.75 \mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules per metal, as sites II−IV begin to populate, the additional \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules affect the rotational potential for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules at site I. This adjusts the rotational energy level, resulting in a shift of the first peak to lower energies and the second peak to higher energies. These peak shifts are similar to those observed for the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) frameworks, with magnitudes of approximately \(0.7 \mathrm{meV}\) for \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\) and \(0.5 \mathrm{meV}\) for \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\text{-dobdc})\), though much less than the \(1.4 \mathrm{meV}\) shift observed for \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\).<sup>4c</sup> Adsorption of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at the secondary sites also results in a significant increase in the area of the peak at \(9 \mathrm{meV}\) and appearance of features near 12 and \(15 \mathrm{meV}\) as a new subset of rotational levels associated with the rotationally hindered second adsorption site. Transitions in this energy range are also observed in spectra reported for higher loadings of the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) materials, indicating the similarity in adsorption potentials at these secondary sites.<sup>4c,6c</sup>
Infrared Spectra. Since the higher binding enthalpies and low-coverage \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake cannot reasonably be attributed to a significant change in the macrostructure of the framework, infrared spectroscopy was used to further probe the binding of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at the open metal coordination sites. The vibrational frequency of adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is almost always lower than that of
the molecule in the gas phase \((4161~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})\), and it is now well established that for metal−organic frameworks, there is a strong correlation between the magnitude of the frequency shift and the binding energy at a particular site.31 A comparison of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) vibrational frequencies is shown at two different \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) loadings for \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\)-dobdc), \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\), \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\)-dobdc), and \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) (Figure 6) and for \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) and \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) (Figure
S45). At this temperature, the pure vibrational part of the spectrum consists of an ortho−para pair that is separated by just \(6~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in the gas phase. The peak near \(4025~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in each spectrum corresponds to the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) bound to the open metal site.
In each case, the spectra for loadings of \(0.5\;\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules per metal, which are known to arise purely from \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) bound to the open metal site, show a greater frequency shift for the \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) than for \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) or \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\). This shift is consistent with the stronger \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding observed in the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) frameworks based on equilibrium adsorption isotherms and neutron diffraction studies. Upon further loading, the secondary adsorption sites begin to be populated, which is evidenced by a second peak that grows in around \(4125~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). The energy of the peaks associated with the secondary sites is very similar to those seen for \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\), consistent with the INS transitions for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) bound to the secondary sites, which were also similar between \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) and \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) compounds. This supports our hypothesis that the electronic structure at the metal center is significantly altered with the \(m\)-dobdc\(^{a}\) linker, leading to a higher initial isosteric heat of adsorption, while the secondary sites in \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) and \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) are similar in binding potential due to structural similarities between the two frameworks. Furthermore, a consistent shift in the frequency for the metal-adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules as a function of concentration is seen for both the \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) samples, although not to the same degree in the \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) sample. These concentration shifts have previously been attributed to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\cdots\mathrm{H}_{2}\) interactions, and it is interesting to note that the shifts seem largely unaffected by the change in linker. Furthermore, these shifts correlate well with the shifts seen in rotational potential as the secondary binding sites are populated in the INS data shown in Figure 5.
Variable-temperature infrared spectroscopy is a standard technique for establishing the enthalpy of adsorption at a particular site.32 Figure 7 shows the spectra obtained for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in
\(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) while lowering the sample temperature from 142 to \(75~\mathrm{K}\). In each case, the initial \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) pressure for the system was set such that only the open metal site was occupied over the full temperature range. The fractional occupancy is then determined by the ratio of the area under the infrared band to that observed at complete saturation. The inset in Figure 7 shows the van’t Hoff relationship plot used to extract both the enthalpy and entropy change upon adsorption. The data obtained in this way for the different materials are summarized in Table 3. The slopes extracted from the van’t Hoff plots are
sensitive to small variations in the maximum saturation area; as such, an error on the order of \(0.5\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) is estimated. Even with these limitations, the data is consistent with the calculated isosteric heats of adsorption from the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms, showing an enthalpy increase from \(\mathrm{Mn}\) to \(\mathrm{Co}\) to \(\mathrm{Ni}\). More importantly, these infrared spectroscopy-based heat of adsorption values are about \(1.4\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) larger than those calculated for their respective \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) counterparts from similar infrared experiments. The binding enthalpy of \(-13.7\,\mathrm{kJ}/\) mol in \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m\)-dobdc) is among the highest reported \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding enthalpies in a metal−organic framework to date. This is consistent with the trends observed using values calculated from \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms. We further note that the measured large entropy changes are consistent with previous studies, in which a strong correlation between the enthalpy and entropy change of the bound hydrogen in different metal−organic frameworks was observed.33 Overall, the redshift seen
in the infrared spectra for the adsorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) as compared to \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) indicates that the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is more strongly bound, and the variable-temperature data confirms the increased binding enthalpies seen from the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption data.
Electronic Structure Calculations. Density functional theory (DFT) was used to examine the differences in the electronic structures at the open metal sites by using representative complexes to model the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) and \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) frameworks. DFT has shown itself to be a fairly robust method for modeling chemical systems; its major failings, self-interaction error and lack of dispersion interactions,34 are well-known and can be corrected for by using appropriate functionals that account for these failings, namely range separation and explicit dispersion correction.35 Previously, the \(\omega\mathrm{B}97\mathrm{X}{-}\mathrm{D}^{36}\) functional was shown to accurately model \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding in metal−organic frameworks.37
Modeling \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) -type frameworks accurately with electronic structure theory is challenging because fragmenting the structure at any point will lead to the neglect of important interactions from the chains formed by the \(\mathbf{M}^{2+}\) ions. In an attempt to understand the nature of the differences in hydrogen binding between \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) and \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) , some accuracy in the calculated energetics is sacrificed in order to learn relevant information about the differences between the two systems. In this vein, our modeling focused on the linker of interest coordinated to two \({\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}\) centers; the remaining ligands on each \({\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}\) ion were truncated as formaldehyde molecules in order to maintain charge balance. Since the key open coordination site for each \({\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}\) center in the framework is enforced by constraints on the ligands imposed by the macrostructure of the framework, which could not be explicitly included in our model, an unconstrained optimized geometry of the model would not actually reflect the structural properties of the framework in question. Thus, the geometry of the linker complex was taken from the experimentally determined crystal structure of the framework and subsequently frozen before the interaction with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) was optimized. Furthermore, the \({\mathrm{Co}}^{\cdots}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\) distance was taken from the neutron diffraction spectra and used as a constraint to ensure an accurate depiction of this interaction in the model system.
While comparing absolute energy differences gives a single number to describe \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding, energy decomposition analysis (EDA) breaks down that number into physically interpretable components. An EDA based on absolutely localized molecular orbitals \((\mathrm{ALMOs})^{21}\) breaks down total binding energies into frozen energy, polarization, and charge-transfer components. The frozen term is due to permanent electrostatics and Pauli repulsions, since the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) electron density is being brought within the van der Waals radius of the Co atom. The polarization term corresponds to the favorable interaction of electrons in the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and complex fragments relaxing in the presence of the other fragment without electron transfer. Charge transfer stems from energy lowering when electrons are allowed to flow from one fragment to the other. A recent generalization of the ALMO EDA to open-shell molecules allows for the application of this method to general metal-containing systems.38
The binding energy decomposition analysis clarifies the differences in these two species (Table 4). Specifically, there is more charge transfer and polarization in the \(m\) -dobdc4− containing complex, which is accompanied by a partially offsetting increase in the energetically unfavorable frozen term. This larger frozen term is the result of the increased steric
repulsion that stems from a shorter \({\mathrm{Co}}^{\cdots}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\) distance in the \(m\) - dobdc4− complex. The increased charge-transfer energy of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding in this species is key, as that is the largest term in the total calculated binding energy. A breakdown of the chargetransfer term can be seen in Table 5; in both cases, the largest
portion of the charge transfer comes from the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) σ orbital to the unoccupied orbitals of the Co complex. The increased \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) to Co forward donation is indicative of more positive charge at the metal cation in the \(m{\bf-}d o b d c^{4-}\) complex as compared with the dobdc4− complex. This explains the experimentally determined increase in binding enthalpy of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\) - dobdc) frameworks as compared with their \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) analogues, as increased positive charge at the metal center leads to stronger polarization of the bound \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) . Additionally, a difference is seen in the location of the bound \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) relative to the linker in the two complexes. In the dobdc4− linker complex, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binds to create a nearly octahedral geometry around the \({\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}\) ion, with a distance of \(3.30\,\mathrm{\AA}\) from the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) to the aromatic carbon bonded to the carboxylate group (the \(\alpha\) carbon). Conversely, in the \(m{\bf-}d o b d c^{4-}\) complex, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is reoriented toward the linker at a distance of \(2.64\mathrm{~\AA~}\) from the \(\alpha\) carbon (Figure 8a,b). This difference suggests that a change in the charge at the \(\alpha\) carbon due to the different symmetry of the \(m\) - dobdc4− linker possibly facilitates a change in the binding geometry for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) . While this significant change may be an artifact associated with the small cluster models, it is the same trend seen experimentally for the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) · \(\alpha\) carbon distance, which is 3.48(3) Å in \({\mathrm{Co}}_{2}(m{\mathrm{-dobdc}})\) and \(3.59(2)\mathrm{~\AA~}\) in \({\mathrm{Co}}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) .
The orbital interactions between \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and the Co complexes provide further insight into the stronger \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding in \({\mathrm{Co}}_{2}(m\mathrm{-}\) dobdc). While increased forward bonding is a product of increased charge density at the metal, the nature of the backbonding from the complex to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is quite different. This can be seen by looking at the complementary occupied virtual orbital pairs (COVPs) that contribute most to backbonding (Figure 8c,d). In the dobdc4− complex, the occupied orbital is primarily localized on the metal center, while in the \(m{\bf-}d o b d c^{4-}\) species, the donating orbital includes contributions from the \(\pi\) system of the linker. This increased backbonding for \(m{\bf-}d o b d c^{4-}\) agrees with the redshift seen in the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) infrared spectra for the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})\) frameworks, as increased backbonding will weaken the \(\mathrm{H-H}\) bond. This possible extra interaction with the linker in the \(m{\bf-}d o b d c^{4-}\) complex, coupled with the larger
forward donation of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) to the more positively charged metal center, are the key differences between these two systems that help to explain the stronger \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding seen in \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\cdot\) - dobdc) versus \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) .
## CONCLUSIONS
The foregoing results demonstrate the synthesis of a new family of metal−organic frameworks, \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\cdot\) -dobdc) \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g},\) Mn, Fe, Co, Ni), representing a potentially less expensive version of their well-known structural isomers \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) . Hydrogen was used as an initial probe to determine how electronic and structural differences in the new frameworks modified the interactions of the open metal coordination sites with adsorbates. The Mn, Fe, Co, and Ni variants exhibited greater isosteric heats of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption by approximately \(1\,\mathrm{\kJ/mol}\) compared to the \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) compounds. The results from powder neutron diffraction, inelastic neutron scattering, and infrared spectroscopy experiments performed on hydrogenloaded samples of \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(m\cdot\) -dobdc) all support this as arising from stronger interactions between the metal centers and the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules. Future efforts will focus on the larger-scale production of \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(m\cdot\) -dobdc) compounds and determining whether the enhanced charge density at the metal sites leads to improvements compared to their \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\) analogues in the efficacy of key gas separations, including \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) from air,1i,39 \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2},^{1\mathrm{h},7\mathrm{a},40}\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2},^{1\mathrm{i},41}\) \(\mathrm{CO/H}_{2},^{1\mathrm{e},39\mathrm{a},42}\) ethylene/ethane,1i,9 propylene/propane,1i,3d,9 and methane purification.43
